Z||000008||ThatRickGuy||06-12-2000||11:40 AM||||Darklord is right. When I got my first car, an '82 Grand Prix, My buddy (the previous owner) took me out to the mall at 2am in the middle of a flooding rain storm. We spent some time going over skid control, slide logic, and how to menuver in slick conditions. I credit most of my slide control skills to that night. The only problem is I've owned 4 old (76-83) pontiacs, with heavy engines up front and no wait in the rear, so making the rear end swing and snap back was simpler, now I just have to find another parking lot and a rain storm for when I pick up my '88 formula. I've heard of a lot of push problems, and also that if you hit the brakes the front will dive and the rear will swing... it'll be an interesting lesson.||||reg||1
Z||000009||stsmithgt||06-12-2000||12:30 PM||||I suppose my foot is a little HEAVY, because the back end of my car seems to always be out there.  Especially in the morning when the streets are wet (coastal weather).  Does anybody have a set of tires there satisfied with that you can also get some miles out of them.  I know there's a comprimize in there, but I would prefer some suggestions on tire selection than to keep buying tires that stick, but wear out too fast or last a long time, but feels like you're driving on an oil slickened surface.  ||||reg||5
Z||000010||Shadow Wolf||06-14-2000||02:39 AM||||If you're on a bit of a budget (but not too strict) see about getting a set of Pacemark directionals.  They have a 225/60R15 all-season "H" rated tire that seems to have really good wet road traction.||||reg||3
Z||000011||BlackGT||06-14-2000||02:48 PM||||I have a very heavy foot as well but I won`t<BR>play with bald tires! The tires I bought for<BR>my toy are Falken ZE 502`s. These tires are <BR>real sticky with a good tread pattern, tire life and stick like gummy bears to your teeth<BR>and not too expensive. $325 a set, I`m running 215/60 front and 225/60 rear and have<BR>yet to break loose on paved roads.||||reg||1
